# 2026-09-18 (session 16): first fire of the cross-catalogue transfer measurement.
#
# WHY: EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md §11 item 1 asks for a prior on hidden-expert-set recall - "emit
# catalogue A (SGMC), score vs independent catalogue B (QFaults), report transfer".  Nothing
# committed can answer it, because the proxy population IS catalogue A: a submission that copies
# catalogue A scores 0.0 there by construction (that is the acceptance control in
# scripts/eval_proxy_catalogue.py, and it is what makes the proxy a fair stand-in for "faults the
# labels lack").  One surrogate cannot say whether a policy TRAVELS between independently compiled
# catalogues or merely fits that catalogue's mapping style.
#
# SOURCE VERIFIED LIVE FROM THIS REPOSITORY'S OWN HTTP REQUESTS (2026-09-18):
#   service  https://earthquake.usgs.gov/arcgis/rest/services/haz/Qfaults/MapServer
#            currentVersion 11.5, mapName "2020 Release", capabilities Map,Query,Data,
#            supportedQueryFormats JSON/geoJSON/PBF, maxRecordCount 2000
#   layer    21 "National Database", esriGeometryPolyline - one query covers the whole nation, so no
#            state-boundary logic and no feature can be missed at a state line (layers 11/4/16 are
#            the Nevada/California/Utah views)
#   footprint EPSG:4326 envelope computed at run time from data/labels.tif:
#            -120.0372, 37.3312, -116.1409, 40.7279 -> the service reported 14,482 intersecting
#            features (returnCountOnly=true)
#   provenance DOI 10.5066/P9BCVRCK, ScienceBase item 589097b1e4b072a7ac0cae23, public domain
#
# WHAT THIS FIRE DOES:
#   job qfaults  - page the query in EPSG:4326 GeoJSON until the fetched count EQUALS the count the
#                  service reported (a silent truncation fails the run rather than shipping a
#                  partial catalogue, which would understate transfer and look like a negative
#                  result), assign each feature a RuleID from the service's OWN renderer vocabulary,
#                  then rasterise with the SAME script that built catalogue A so the codes mean the
#                  same thing (0 none / 1 already within R of a training label / 2 not).
#   job transfer - controls FIRST (labels-copy must score ~0 against B-only, B-copy must score 1.0,
#                  blanket-ones gives the trivial floor), then emit A's code-2 pixels at widths
#                  0..6 px, score every emission against B's code-2 pixels with the official metric,
#                  and test whether union(model, A) - a probability maximum, not a mask OR - beats
#                  the model alone by more than the pre-registered margin with pre-registered
#                  bootstrap support.  The verdict is DERIVED: "ADOPT" is unreachable if a control
#                  fails, and "REFUSED" is printed instead of a number nobody may act on.
#
# Egress: earthquake.usgs.gov is not reachable from the development sandbox (verified 2026-09-18,
# curl exit 35) but is reachable from a GitHub-hosted runner - the same constraint that put the SGMC
# fetch and the competition-data bridge on runners.
#
# Parameters: .github/triggers/cross-catalogue-params

